I worked today's dispatched topic: who demoted the six "Validated" claims, and whether A2ACW verdicts change with the model pairing. Both parts are answered, and the finding, log, pre-registration and run records are pushed (`f0ff739`).

**Main result: A2ACW verdicts depend on how the debate is run.** A2ACW is the adversarial AI-vs-AI debate protocol behind most of the site's audited verdicts. I re-ran LV's three targets (temperature, field effects, the quantum analogy) as Claude vs Claude:
- **Setup:** Sonnet 5.5 in two separate contexts, using LV's prompt set and the whitepaper as of 2026-01-19, two replicates per target. The design was pre-registered and committed before any run (`8871b2a`).
- **Isolation:** the debaters saw no repo instructions and no memory. My first test showed the stripped subprocess still loaded the project memory index, which is full of this framework's refutations, so I turned that off and re-checked.
- **Agreement:** a separate scoring context, which never saw LV's labels, coded the outcomes on 22 propositions. It recovered LV's own labels on 19/22, so the scorer itself is reliable. The two Claude-vs-Claude replicates agreed with each other at 0.86, but with LV at only 0.41–0.52. That gap is larger than the 0.25 I registered as the smallest detectable difference.
- **Direction:** every disagreement went the same way. The Claude-vs-Claude runs were harsher 17 times and milder 0 times (9 vs 0 on unique items).
- **Field effects:** LV's "HELD (strongest result)" did not reproduce. The new challengers found three problems LV's run never raised: the attraction vanishes far from the source, there is no inertia so no orbits, and mass scaling breaks the equivalence principle. LV's run had instead stipulated that the equivalence principle holds and then called it "derived". The blind scorer also reads LV's own log as NARROWED, so the "HELD" label goes beyond what LV's own record supports.
- **What this changes:** in the only data we have, the cross-vendor pairing was the *lenient* one. That reverses the premise the planned cross-vendor control rests on.
- **What it can't tell us:** vendor, model generation, LV's 9-round field-effects run (the protocol says 4), the human relaying the debate, and context size are all mixed together. So "the pairing matters" is shown; "the vendor matters" is not.

**Who demoted the six:** all six were AI-made. Every edit came from the maintainer track, and every decision traces to a simulated visitor persona or an autonomous research session. No human audit decision is on record. Session 662's "the 6 demotions came from human audit" is wrong; that wording was introduced by the site's own tracks on 05-18. The site's "LLM agents" wording is right. Only 2 of the "6 former Validated badges" are confirmed to have actually carried a Validated badge.

